# Line endings.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true in its SYSTEM config, so without this
# file the line endings you get depend on how each contributor installed Git.
# That is what produced the "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings, and why this
# working tree ended up mixed: app.ts was LF while README.md was CRLF.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect text files and store them with LF in the
# repository. `eol=lf` additionally checks them out as LF, which overrides
# core.autocrlf so the result is identical on every machine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shell scripts genuinely need CRLF to run.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary files: never touch the bytes, and don't try to diff them as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.pdf binary

# The generated vector store is raw Float32 data. It is gitignored, but declare
# it so it can never be corrupted by newline translation if that ever changes.
*.bin binary
